'''Lurandian Football''' is a code of [[w:Gridiron football|gridiron football]] that originated in [[Lurandia]], a continent in [[Adonia]], during the latter half of the 19th century. Developed from the broader family of handling, running, and kicking ball sports established in northern and western Adonia, Lurandian Football evolved into the planet's preeminent gridiron discipline through its own distinct institutional codification. The sport is contested by two opposing squads of twelve players on an expansive rectangular turf surface measuring 110 yards by 65 yards, bounded by 20-yard-deep end zones at either terminal, with the ultimate objective of advancing an oblate spheroid ball into the opponent's territory to accumulate points.

The core regulatory framework of Lurandian Football is defined by high tactical velocity, structural spatial width, and constant physical confrontation. Matches are contested across three 20-minute periods termed Thirds, with a compressed 20-second play clock and a concluding three-minute Dead-Time clock in the final Third. Offenses operate under a strict '''three-down''' sequence to gain twelve yards (the Three-and-Twelve system), with a conditional Breakthrough Fourth Down unlocked if nine yards are achieved over the first two attempts. Offensive lines and defenses are separated by a 1.5-yard Neutral Corridor that requires upright stances and outlaws low cut-blocking, while backfield personnel are granted unlimited simultaneous forward pre-snap motion. Passing rules permit Dual Forward Releases behind the line of scrimmage as well as Protected Lateral pitch chains downfield. The kicking phase incorporates the historic single-point [[Rouge (Lurandian Football)|rouge]], open Return Punts, and Onside Chasers, with returners guarded by a mandatory five-yard spatial cushion rather than a fair catch mechanism. Squads must also field two designated Two-Way Anchors per Third, maintaining the sport's traditional ethos of complete conditioning.

The global administrative authority for the code is the '''[[Lurandian Football Union]]''' (LFU), established in 1910 and based in [[Nanaimo]], [[Kaneda]]. Across the continent of Lurandia, the sport commands premier cultural standing and constitutes the most-watched athletic enterprise in [[Kaneda]] and [[Kazabune]]. The code has also expanded internationally across Adonia, achieving massive prominence in [[Okaiken]]—where it is culturally embraced under the designation {{nihongo|鎧球|Gaikyū|armor ball}}—and sustaining an active competitive circuit in the [[United Midlands]].

==History==

===Origins in Kaneda (1860s–1890s)===
The origins of Lurandian Football are rooted in the maritime and industrial hubs of [[Kaneda]], the primary nation of the Lurandian landmass. Handling codes and kicking games were introduced to Kanedan educational institutions through regional exchanges during the mid-19th century, leading to informal campus competitions throughout [[Nanaimo]] and [[Kanakou]] by the late 1860s.

During the 1870s, athletic clubs began departing from traditional continuous play by instituting structured stoppages, formal lines of scrimmage, and an orderly system of possession attempts. The pivotal convention establishing a three-down sequence occurred in 1878 under the authority of the Nanaimo Collegiate Athletic Association. The rationale behind restricting possession series to three attempts stemmed directly from the expansive proportions of the Kanedan playing grounds: club directors maintained that granting more attempts over a wide field encouraged slow, defensive deadlocks, whereas a concise three-down sequence preserved high offensive velocity and demanded exceptional downfield precision.

Intercollegiate circuits flourished throughout the 1880s, marked by the inaugural recognized intercollegiate championship match in 1882 between the University of Nanaimo and Kanakou College. The sport spread rapidly along commercial railway lines, finding dedicated followings among university scholars, factory workers, and civic athletic societies.

===Codification and the Lurandian Football Union (1910)===
[[File:Lurandian Football historical 1920s.jpg|thumb|right|280px|An early Lurandian Football squad in Nanaimo, Kaneda (c. 1926), showing players in leather headgear and quilted canvas pants.]]
Disparate regional regulations across Kaneda created recurring friction during inter-provincial matches toward the turn of the century. Variations in goal-line rules, timing structures, and kicking resolutions hampered inter-regional play, prompting civic leaders to pursue universal harmonization.

The '''[[Lurandian Football Union]]''' was formally chartered in 1910 during a general congress of fourteen regional sporting associations convening in Nanaimo. The assembly established the canonical statutory standard that continues to define the game: twelve active players per side, a 110-by-65-yard field featuring 20-yard end zones, upright goal posts erected directly upon the goal line, and the formal inclusion of the single-point rouge.

The convention also confirmed the outright prohibition of the fair catch. Seeking to preserve the tactical drama of every kicking sequence while safeguarding vulnerable returners, the union mandated that defensive pursuit players maintain an unobstructed five-yard cushion until the receiver secured possession.

===Professional era and the Continental League (1925–present)===
The emergence of professional organizations began in earnest with the founding of the [[Kanedan Professional Football League]] (KPFL) in 1925, launching with eight charter franchises based in major manufacturing and shipping cities. The physical intensity and strategic choreography of the sport drew massive working-class audiences during the interwar era.

In neighboring Kazabune, a parallel league network developed through the 1930s, culminating in regular cross-border exhibition series. Widespread public enthusiasm led to the creation of the binational [[Lurandian Continental League]] (LCL) in 1948, integrating the top tier of Kanedan and Kazabune clubs into a unified competitive pyramid. The annual championship fixture, known as the '''Grey Shield''', swiftly became the most watched athletic event on the continent.

Following the conclusion of the [[Great Adonian War]], the sport achieved significant international dispersion. Kanedan personnel stationed in [[Okaiken]] during the post-war reconstruction period introduced the discipline to Sanese university athletes and industrial workers in 1946. What began as informal base fixtures evolved into a nationwide cultural phenomenon in Okaiken, while parallel development programs introduced the code to maritime centers across the [[United Midlands]]. The inaugural quadrennial [[Lurandian Football Championship]] was staged in 1978, formalizing international representative competition across Adonia.

==Rules==

===Match structure and timing===
Lurandian Football is contested over sixty minutes of regulation divided into three 20-minute periods designated as '''Thirds'''. The tripartite division reflects the sport's symbolic "Rule of Three" (three periods, three downs, three attempts) and requires squads to alternate defending end zones after each twenty-minute interval, balancing the tactical influence of coastal winds and weather conditions.

The interval between plays is governed by a compressed '''20-second play clock''' initiated immediately upon the head referee's ready-for-play signal. Failure to center the ball within twenty seconds results in an automatic five-yard delay of game infraction.

To eliminate tactical stalling and prevent teams from kneeling out the clock, the final three minutes of the third period are governed by the '''Dead-Time Clock Rule'''. During this concluding window, the game clock halts on every tackle in bounds, run out of bounds, incomplete pass, change of possession, or penalty assessment, restarting only when the ball is centered on the subsequent snap. Leading teams cannot run out the clock without actively executing offensive plays.

===Field and dimensions===
Lurandian Football is contested on an expansive rectangular turf surface measuring 110 yards (100.6 m) in length between goal lines and 65 yards (59.4 m) in total width. At each end, the playing boundary extends into a deep end zone measuring 20 yards (18.3 m), creating an aggregate surface area of 150 yards (137.2 m) from end line to end line. White transverse lines traverse the surface at five-yard intervals, intersected by yard line numerals every ten yards, while longitudinal hash marks designate the inner boundary where scrimmage plays must initiate.

The goal posts are situated directly above the front goal line. The crossbar stands 10 feet (3.05 m) above the turf, supporting two vertical uprights spaced 18 feet 6 inches (5.64 m) apart. The expansive width of the 65-yard boundary and the generous depth of the end zones provide substantial running perimeter and extensive passing corridors, setting the spatial conditions for the sport's high-speed movement.

===Players and the Two-Way Anchor rule===
Twelve players represent each squad on the field during active play. Offensive formations feature five offensive linemen (a center, two guards, and two offensive tackles), an orchestrating quarterback, a primary ball carrier (halfback or fullback), and five perimeter receivers comprising split ends, flankers, tight ends, and slotbacks. Defenses counter with varied combinations of interior defensive tackles, edge rushers, linebackers, cornerbacks, defensive halfbacks, and safeties.

To preserve the traditional ethos of complete conditioning and prevent excessive platoon substitution, each matchday roster must designate two '''Two-Way Anchors''' for each twenty-minute Third. These two players must remain on the field for both offensive and defensive possessions throughout that period—such as a slotback who transitions to free safety, or a tight end who steps into an edge-rushing linebacker role.

===Line of scrimmage and the Neutral Corridor===
The offensive and defensive lines are separated prior to the snap by an expanded '''1.5-yard Neutral Corridor''' (4.5 feet). Defensive linemen are required to align in two-point upright or staggered semi-crouch stances outside this corridor. When the ball is centered, defensive rushers must cross this space with momentum, turning line-of-scrimmage collisions into contests of speed, agility, and hand placement rather than static helmet-to-helmet grinding.

All forms of blocking below the waist—including chop blocks, cut blocks, and crackback blocks—are illegal across the entirety of the playing surface. Offensive linemen must initiate contact above the waist and within the frame of the opponent's torso using open hands.

===Downs and the Breakthrough Down===
Possession is governed by the '''Three-and-Twelve system'''. An offensive squad receives three downs to advance the football a minimum of twelve yards toward the opponent's goal line. 

If an offense gains at least nine yards across its first two attempts without crossing the twelve-yard line to gain, the unit is awarded an optional Fourth Down, termed the '''Breakthrough Down'''. An offense that gains eight yards or fewer across its first two attempts remains strictly restricted to three downs and must either punt, attempt a field goal, or risk an immediate turnover on downs at the dead-ball spot.

===Passing and ball-advancement dynamics===
Eligible backfield personnel are permitted '''unlimited simultaneous forward pre-snap motion''', accelerating toward the line of scrimmage prior to the snap provided they remain behind the neutral zone when the ball is centered.

Offenses may execute up to two forward passes on a single scrimmage play, known as '''Dual Forward Releases''', provided both throws are initiated from behind the original line of scrimmage. A quarterback may throw a quick forward pass to a flat receiver behind the line, who may then halt and launch a secondary downfield pass.

Open-field lateral pitching beyond the line of scrimmage is protected by the '''Protected Lateral Rule'''. If an uncontested backward pitch strikes the turf without being touched by a defender, the play is whistled dead immediately at the spot of the bounce, with the offense retaining possession at the point of the bounce with loss of yardage. If a defender intercepts the backward pass in flight, the ball remains live and may be returned.

===Scoring===
Points are accumulated through eight recognized scoring actions during competitive regulation:

