|+ Scoring methods in Lurandian Football
|-
! Method !! Points !! Description
|-
| [[w:Touchdown|Touchdown (The Breach)]] || 6 || Ball carried across the goal line or caught in the 20-yard end zone
|-
| Tier 1 Conversion (kick) || 1 || Ball placekicked through the uprights from the 15-yard line following a touchdown
|-
| Tier 2 Conversion (play or drop-kick) || 2 || Ball advanced into the end zone from the 3-yard line via run or pass, or drop-kicked from the 25-yard line
|-
| Tier 3 High Conversion || 3 || Ball advanced into the end zone from the 10-yard line via run or pass following a touchdown
|-
| Standard Field Goal || 3 || Ball placekicked through the uprights from inside the 45-yard line during active scrimmage play
|-
| Cannon Goal || 4 || Ball placekicked through the uprights from 45 yards or greater (measured from the spot of the kick)
|-
| Open-Play Drop-Kick || 4 || Ball drop-kicked through the uprights from active scrimmage on any down, regardless of distance
|-
| [[w:Safety (gridiron football score)|Safety]] || 2 || Ball carrier tackled within their own end zone or foul committed by offense in end zone
|-
| [[Rouge (Lurandian Football)|Rouge (Single)]] || 1 || Kicked ball downed in opponent's end zone or kicked entirely through and out of the end zone
|-
| Iron Stop || 1 || Defending team forcing a turnover on downs inside its own 5-yard line
|}

The '''rouge''', also known as the single point, plays an instrumental role in late-game execution. If a punter kicks the ball into the defending end zone and the receiving unit fails to advance it out across the goal line into field play, the kicking team receives one point. A rouge is also scored on a missed field goal that crosses the dead-ball line or remains unreturned from the end zone. 

The '''Iron Stop''' directly reflects the cultural integration of defensive fortitude, awarding one point to a defending squad that successfully halts an opposing offensive drive inside its own five-yard line on third or fourth down.

===Kicking play, Return Punts, and Onside Chasers===
Lurandian Football does not permit a fair catch stoppage. Instead, all members of the kicking team must yield an unobstructed '''five-yard cushion'''—termed the no-yards zone—around the punt returner until the ball has been physically contacted. Encroaching within the five-yard radius prior to contact constitutes a no-yards violation, incurring an immediate fifteen-yard penalty if the ball is gathered in the air, or a five-yard penalty if the ball was gathered off the turf.

Receiving returners trapped deep in their own territory may execute a '''Return Punt''', drop-kicking or punting the ball back downfield during the return. If the return kick travels into the opposing end zone and remains unreturned, the returning squad scores a counter-rouge.

Any kicking team player who is positioned behind the punter at the precise instant the ball is kicked is designated an '''Onside Chaser'''. An onside chaser may legally recover a kicked ball downfield, securing immediate offensive possession without requiring a touch by the receiving team.

===Overtime===
Matches tied at the expiration of sixty regulation minutes enter a sudden-victory extra period. Possessions begin at the opponent's 35-yard line, with alternating offensive opportunities. The first team to register a score by any legal means—touchdown, field goal, rouge, or safety—secures immediate victory, terminating the contest.

